## Building the mesh, not rebuilding it

These are technique rules for authored geometry — what the
sanctioned `blended.ops` API supports. Each carries the number that
measured it, because "overlap shells" teaches nothing and "butting
welds axis-centre vertices into one vertex on 40 faces" teaches the
shape of the failure. Imported-geometry rules (weld-before-decimate,
visibility culling, tris-to-quads, retopology, region protection)
live in the ingest lane, not here.

## Construction

- Overlap shells, never butt them. Butting welds axis-centre vertices into
  pinched soup (one vertex on 40 faces); N primitives should give N islands
  unless you meant them to weld.
- A weld is an ingest step for imported soup, not cleanup for authored
  layers — blanket-welding fused layered shells into 88 non-manifold
  junctions.
- A centred-section sweep must clear inner_shell_outer + thickness/2, not
  inner_shell_outer — arithmetic, not taste.

## Measure and report

- Read the EVALUATED mesh when modifiers are in play: a modifier closes
  boundaries and doubles the vertex count, so any vertex-count ratio must
  be density-invariant.
- A position filter cannot name a feature: max(loops, key=len) picked the
  collar over the hem. Name features structurally.
- Three appearance metrics, never averaged into one: silhouette IoU catches
  deleted or shrunken parts, shading RMSE catches faceting, and a coverage
  metric catches poke-through. They fail independently and a single number
  hides all three.
- Prefer the data API to bpy.ops: operators need an active area, a selection
  and the linked view layer, and fail differently headless.